  "textBooks": {
    "yms_year": 114.0,
    "yms_smester": 1.0,
    "cls_id": "CB25120",
    "sub_id": "13919",
    "scr_dup": "001",
    "tpb_seq": 1,
    "tpb_book": null,
    "tpb_book_en": "<div>\n<div>Weingast, Barry and Wittman, Donald. (2006). The Oxford Handbook of Political Economy. Oxford: Oxford University Press.</div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div>Krugman, Paul and Wells, Robin. (2018). Macroeconomics. New York: Worth Publishers.</div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div>Kennedy, Paul. (1989). The Rise and Fall of the Great Powers. New York: Random House.</div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div>Marshall, Tim. (2015). Prisoners of Geography: Ten Maps That Tell You Everything You Need to Know About Global Politics. London: Elliot and Thompson Limited.</div>\n</div>\n"
  },
  "readings": {
    "yms_year": 114.0,
    "yms_smester": 1.0,
    "cls_id": "CB25120",
    "sub_id": "13919",
    "scr_dup": "001",
    "tpc_seq": 1,
    "tpc_book": null,
    "tpc_book_en": "<div>These books offer an overview of the most important areas of political economy. It is truly interdisciplinary, overlaping not only politicis and economics but also sociology, history, geography etc. Only after studying all these different aspects can we truly understand how a country&#39;s politics and economy function in liu with each other.</div>\n"
  },
